      "title": "Collectors and Revivals: Saving Oral Tradition",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** history | **Difficulty:** 3/10 | **Time:** ~10 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Field collectors preserved oral-tradition music that might otherwise have been lost, shaping revival taste through what they published."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-figures",
      "title": "Key figures",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Francis O'Neill** (1848–1936): Chicago police chief who published *The Music of Ireland* (1903) and *O'Neill's 1001* — thousands of tunes from Irish-American musicians. **Cecil Sharp** (1859–1924): documented English morris and country dances. **Alan Lomax** (1915–2002): recorded Appalachian ballads, blues, and global folk across decades."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "critical-reading",
      "title": "Critical reading",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Collectors shaped versions through selection and harmonisation. Titles drift; keys may not match local practice. Living players remember alternate turns."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "modern-chain",
      "title": "Modern chain",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Kitchen player → collector → digital archive → your tune book. Cite sources; stay open to alternate versions."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
